{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff","id":1552894,"title":"How does one recognize a Caitiff?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-04T18:45:13-07:00","id":1552894,"title":"How does one recognize a Caitiff?","content":"For my upcoming chronicle, there is a PC who will be playing a Caitiff. In terms of his backstory, he was illegally embraced as part of some ploy. He doesn't know who his sire is. However, he quickly realized he needed to get out of his current city and is trying to set himself elsewhere.<br /> <br /> This kind of backstory isn't actually that rare in Vampire chronicles. It's almost the default background for Gangrel. It happens to a lot of Brujah characters too. Eventually those characters are recognized as being from their clan and accepted by them as clanmates. For some of the other clans, this presents more problems, but it is not impossible - perhaps not even improbable - for such characters to be accepted as Ventrue or Toreador. Maybe even Tremere. Obviously, both Malkavians and Nosferatu should be able to recognize their clanmates without much issue because there are objective signs that will lead to that (physical appearance for Nosferatu, and a link to the Madness Network/Cobweb for Malkavians - although I'll note without the Malkavian Time ability, they might not be detected). Such characters might first be recognized as social caitiffs (outcasts and exiles from their clans), but eventually their actual connection to the clan will be identified (they are recognized as not game mechanic Caitiff with no clan weakness and no clan disciplines).<br /> <br /> But what about actual Caitiffs (clanless)? At what point (and how) do vampires determine someone is actually &quot;clanless&quot; and not just an unidentified member of a real clan?<br /> <br /> With some exceptions (like Malkavians and Nosferatu), it probably is not by clan weakness. Not only do many clan weaknesses be hard to tell off hand (or not show up for a long time), some clan weaknesses are duplicated by Flaws any vampire could potentially have. Is that guy who is prone to frenzy a Brujah, a Ventrue with the Short Fuse flaw, or perhaps someone who just has low Self-Control and Willpower? Is she a Ventrue or a Toreador with the Prey Exclusion flaw? And so on.<br /> <br /> So what gives it away? Why is Gordon Keaton from Chicago By Night 1e considered to be a Caitiff and not a Brujah given his sire is known to be Dooley and Dooley's other childe Anita is accepted as a Brujah?<br /> <br /> It is that his initial Disciplines don't seem to be Brujah clan Disciplines (Keaton has mostly Dominate and Auspex; Anita has all three Brujah clan Disciplines)? But what about Jimmy Holcombe (Neon) whose sire was Balthazar? Neon's only Discipline is Celerity which is a Brujah clan discipline. So why would people think he's simply a Caitiff as opposed to &quot;clan unknown&quot;? (Or maybe in Neon's case we should roleplay it like others simply think he's clan unknown until they realize it later.) One could say the same thing about Maldavis who shares Discipline combinations that could be assigned to multiple clans (other than the fact that as Caitiff she is presumably spending XP according to the Caitiff Discipline XP track, Carol could easily be mechanically what she is socially - an illegally made and abandoned Tremere who was never bonded to the Inner Circle and never taught Thaumaturgy). So is it a combination of their starting Disciplines and possibly what Disciplines their childer demonstrate? Is this something that may take years to determine?<br /> <br /> What do others think?<br /> <br /> I think this is important to figure out. For while some clans - like Ventrue or Toreador - may exclude such characters from their clan gatherings and social life until it becomes &quot;obvious&quot; who they are, there will also be clans - like Brujah or Gangrel - who will less exclusionary UNTIL they learn their real clan or if they are actually clanless.<br /> <br /> For my chronicle purposes, while my players and I know the PC is Caitiff, I'm thinking the NPCs might just simply put him into the category of &quot;unknown clan, we'll figure it out later&quot;. At that point, some clans might be more accepting than others. Then at some point, the character is recognized as &quot;Caitiff&quot; and any social stigma associated with that really kicks in. &quot;What is this PCs's actual clan?&quot; might therefore be a real &quot;mystery&quot; in the chronicle for a while before the NPCs decide he truly is Caitiff.<br /> <br /> I'm still trying to decide how to play this out in game. I think everyone simply deciding he is Caitiff from the beginning is probably the most boring option, but interested in other people's opinions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-04T20:29:49-07:00","id":1552902,"title":"","content":"So, there's a number of things to unpack, here.<br /> <br /> 1- What defines a <b>Caitiff</b> is the lack of mystical ties with the clan. It is literally the game effects of having no Clan Weakness and no In-Clans, instead buying every Discipline at a middle-point cost;<br /> <br /> 2- Most of the time, the social aspect overlaps with the mystical aspect. In short, <b>Caitiff</b> will almost always be vampires with unknown sire, not even a sire that watches them from afar. There are exceptions on both sides, though, a lot of vampires with unknown sires have Clan traits, so they're not <b>Caitiff</b>, and a few <b>Caitiff</b> have known sires, and even some were adequately raised by their sires, but for whatever reason it &quot;didn't stick&quot;;<br /> <br /> 3- Only a few clans and bloodlines really have the means to readily identify someone isn't from their clan, much less a <b>Caitiff</b>, and the <b>Malkavian</b> actually aren't among them. From the Clans, old and new, only the <b>Nosferatu</b> can objectively tell someone isn't from their clan without magic, and even then only before 5th edition, now you can't even tell the difference between a Nos and someone really ugly;<br /> <br /> 4- The <b>Tremere</b> can divine someone's clan with magic, and <u>only them</u> can do so. Even other sorcerer vampires don't have access to this specific ritual. On the other hand, though, it is a rather common ritual among the <b>Tremere</b> and most chantries have at least one member that can cast it and offer as a service. On top of that, their hierarchy and practices mean that most <b>Tremere</b> have actually went through this very ritual, so passing as one of them isn't an easy task. It is doable, though;<br /> <br /> 5- In the end, recognizing a <b>Caitiff</b> is a matter of long time observation, with a number of errors in the way. Most <b>Caitiffs</b> are &quot;identified&quot; simply by not having a known sire and not knowing anything at first about the vampiric society. If with time they consistently show the telltales of a given clan, they may be &quot;uplifted&quot; to full clan status, simply because calling them <b>Caitiff</b> was an error in the first place;<br /> <br /> 6- As such, if a <b>Caitiff</b> tries to pass as a Clan vampire, they can actually pull the stunt for a long time, maybe forever, depending only on how well they perform.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-04T23:46:58-07:00","id":1552911,"title":"","content":"I think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> has covered a lot of the 'How' questions. But I do think that there's a couple of interesting possibilities raised by the question that are worth exploring:<br /> <br /> 1) It could be considered an error of judgment to use the word 'Caitiff' to refer to both a social/informational condition and a separate biological/physiological/??? condition. It's particularly noteworthy that, as mentioned above, social-Caitiff can eventually be linked to a Clan even if you never identify their sire, while a physiological-Caitiff can be such even when you know who their sire is.<br /> <br /> 2) Is it possible that there is, in fact, a correlation and/or causal relationship between being a social-Caitiff and a physiological-Caitiff? It would make sense to use the same term for both if the two conditions often overlap, even if exceptions exist. It makes me wonder if something akin to a sympathetic connection influences the development of Clan traits. If I recall correctly, Clan traits generally develop over time after the Embrace, rather than immediately, which might support the idea that proximity to the sire might influence the expression of Clan traits. There would probably be other complicating factors, or else it would be a bit more obvious, but it would make sense how the two conditions would be conflated if there was a lot of overlap between them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-05T00:43:57-07:00","id":1552912,"title":"","content":"As much as it important in the setting regardless of edition the creation of Caitiff never really were defined and set in stone<br /> A Caitiff is someone who does not belong to any established clan thus often referred to as &quot;clanless&quot;.<br /> This can be due to a variety of reasons, including a flawed or unknown lineage, being abandoned by their sire, or their blood being too weak to manifest clan-specific traits.<br /> The vast majority of Caitiff bear the term in a purely political sense and most of the Kindred's history, the terms &quot;Caitiff&quot; and &quot;Thin-Blood&quot; were used interchangeably<br /> A Caitiff Vitae contains the ties to a clan (a Brujah who leave their child behind would be still a Brujah logically), but due to their lineage being unclear for too long they never were able to establish themselves in the structure of a Clan, and therefore lack that Clan's support.<br /> It's a rare few who end up without any particularity to their Vitae, who are truly Clanless.<br /> As far as those rare few go, the truth about becoming a Caitiff is that no one really knows how exactly it happens.<br /> The process of becoming a Vampire is not instantaneous, for example Ventrue can take a few weeks for their feeding preference to solidify, and Nosferatu can take several days to even weeks for their physical transformation to complete and even the Malkavian maddness not instantly obvious or kicks in.<br /> If a Vampire goes too long without learning of their Sire's Clan, sometimes it causes them not to develop the particularities of the Vitae that should've come with a Clan. Sometimes, though, even with their lineage being clear, sometimes the Vitae just does that, and you just end up a Caitiff for seemingly no real reason whatsoever.<br /> So we have two types of Caitiff: social outcast and physical/spiritual/Mysical anomalies.<br /> <br /> This presents an interesting situation:<br /> If a Sire with a Clan abbandons its Child at Embrace that child became a Caitiff and despite her Sire had a clan, the lack of interaction barrs them to develop a &quot;Clan&quot;?<br /> Good question<br /> And most of the time the answer is a yes... no... maybe?<br /> <br /> It seems most of the time the Child inherite some &quot;residue&quot; Clan traits, or will become a Child with all the inhertiance of a Clan/blodline (Clan flaws, disciplines, etc) or will be a blank slate - or sometims branch out to be a Bloodline with an affinity to a certain Discipline and/or a falw of thei own<br /> I think Dr. Douglas Netchurch made some experiments on this, like switching two freshly Embraced Child's Sire, and observed their developement and their affinites and so on<br /> <br /> Generally speaking it seem having a Sire around who teach you and simply be around of him and your Clan molds the Child to be the member of the Clan, helps the Vitae to develope so to speak, akin to a mystical resonance that tunes up the Vitae to a certain (eg. Clan) &quot;frequency&quot;<br /> Yet there are many exaples this not really happens, like the Gangrel who not really keep an eye on their Child or very rarely watching them from a distance, yet they are Gangrel from day 1<br /> <br /> So in this case the main question is: what kind of Caitiff we are talking about?<br /> Social or mystical?<br /> And build from up there in my humble oppinion","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-05T09:56:04-07:00","id":1552919,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552912#post1552912\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">most of the Kindred's history, the terms &quot;Caitiff&quot; and &quot;Thin-Blood&quot; were used interchangeably</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not completely true.<br /> <br /> While there's a strong correlation between <b>Caitiff</b> and &quot;<b>Thin-Bloods</b>&quot;, it isn't direct, 1:1. They overlap a lot, but both can also happen in isolation, and that has always been the case.<br /> <br /> It's also curious that both always existed in Kindred society, focusing on the highest <i><b>commonly occurring</b></i> Generation at the time. There was a time when 6th Gen vampires were regularly <b>Caitiff</b> and/or <b>Thin-Bloods</b>. It's unclear if they just died out since them, or changed to normal vampires over time. What can be said for sure that also differentiates them is that <b>Thin-Bloods</b> completely stop occurring at a given Generation as new Generations arrive, while <b>Caitiff</b> can still exist at any Generation, they're just rarer in the lower ones.<br /> <br /> Also, and that was my point in my post, while <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25886\" data-vbnamecard=\"25886\">Kharnov</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a> are right that there are &quot;social&quot; and &quot;mystical&quot; <b>Caitiff</b>, the definition itself is only for the mystical. Social <b>Caitiffs</b> come more from the suspicion that someone is a <b>Caitiff</b>. Sure, not every vampire has a firm grasp on this definition, and on many Domains the very fact that you can't say who's your sire is a problem regardless. But the whole idea behind calling someone a <b>Caitiff</b> is the supposition that they don't carry the signs of their clan.<br /> <br /> Once a <b>Caitiff</b> is knowledgeable enough to pass as a clan vampire, they would know if they're actually from a clan, just with unknown sire, and at this point assuming this clan isn't wrong (or hard) even if they don't know their sire. So the very question as presented only makes sense for a &quot;mystical&quot; <b>Caitiff</b> anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-05T11:09:33-07:00","id":1552920,"title":"","content":"If I remember correctly is that most caitiffs lack defined characteristics of the 13 clans and learning Disciplines are harder for them in general. At least for mystical reasons in general and even if they were sired by a member of a known clan at all and for some reason don't gain the strengths and weaknesses of the clan.<br /> <br /> For social reasons it simply class reasons as they are viewed closer to insects then proper kindred.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-05T13:08:39-07:00","id":1552928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552902#post1552902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">5- In the end, recognizing a <b>Caitiff</b> is a matter of long time observation, with a number of errors in the way. Most <b>Caitiffs</b> are &quot;identified&quot; simply by not having a known sire and not knowing anything at first about the vampiric society. If with time they consistently show the telltales of a given clan, they may be &quot;uplifted&quot; to full clan status, simply because calling them <b>Caitiff</b> was an error in the first place</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that this is probably the case in general. However, what exactly are these &quot;telltales&quot;?<br /> <br /> Clan weakness is actually not that good of a tell because so many Flaws replicate them. This could mean that in the setting their might be several upstanding &quot;clan&quot; members who actually aren't members of the clan.<br /> <br /> Now you could go with the idea that besides showing the clan weakness herself, vampires also look to see whether her childer also show the clan weakness. If they do, they were right in accepting her to their clan. If they don't, then it might be the case her childer are simpler true Caitiff, but she is still of the clan. Or it might be seen that she actually is a true Caitiff and &quot;downgraded&quot; again. It all depends on her clanmates.<br /> <br /> I don't have a problem with that. It's just that we never see any example of such things (probably due to a lack of imagination of the writers combined with them not wanting to confuse readers in a too convoluted backstory).<br /> <br /> The only other telltale I can think of is their initial Disciplines. If the first Disciplines they develop don't match the expected in-clans, that's probably a good sign. Not perfect, but good especially if they compare that with something that matches the clan weakness.<br /> <br /> That would work if one knows one's sire, and the sire is looking after their childe. If they see too many deviances, they realize their childe is actually a true Caitiff.<br /> <br /> However, for people who don't know who their sire is, others can't use that. Many combinations of Disciplines might point to a certain clan origin which may or may not be true.<br /> <br /> So how do other Kindred react during this time period? Just treat everyone who does not have a known sire as Caitiff? This is something a Ventrue or Toreador might do, but I think (at least some, maybe most) Brujah and Gangrel may give someone the benefit of the doubt. Someone who has an unknown sire, but shows one of their clan Discipline? We'll accept that guy with Potence as Brujah... until we know better. Same with Gangrel and one of their Disciplines (An, Animalism, the true blood shows!). Many clans may default to &quot;Caitiff until proven otherwise&quot;, but perhaps not all.<br /> <br /> Are there any other possible telltales that I am missing? Or is it just Clan Weakness and initial Disciplines?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-05T15:41:28-07:00","id":1552933,"title":"","content":"I suspect that in the end whether one is treated as Caitiff or Clan boils down to how the Clan Elders treat you - the latter having long practice and long experience, their juniors will trust their judgement on the matter (or at least have serious reservations about contradicting the Old Guard where the latter can catch them out or snatch them up).<br /> <br /> As for the Elders themselves, I suspect that simple gut instinct (accurate or inaccurate) plays a role: if the Childe <i>feels</i> like one of the Clan then they will be treated as one of the Clan (Especially if they work to make themselves agreeable to their Clanmates and work to fit in).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-05T19:49:41-07:00","id":1552939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552920#post1552920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I remember correctly is that most caitiffs lack defined characteristics of the 13 clans and learning Disciplines are harder for them in general.</div> </div> </div> </div>They may still have minor forms of the clan's characteristics, that's pretty common.<br /> <br /> On the Disciplines, they actually have an easier, not a harder time. And that's a large part of why they're feared and persecuted.<br /> <br /> Normal vampires pay x5 for In-clans and x7 for Out-of-Clans. <b>Caitiff</b> pay x6 for everything, which is a slower start, but a better prospect in the long run. They also have access to learning more Disciplines without direct contact with other users, potentially learning anything as if it was a physical Discipline, even exclusive or secret Disciplines. That's at the ST's discretion, obviously, but's still possible to see a <b>Caitiff</b> spontaneously learning anything. Finally, they have a great facility at creating completely new Disciplines other vampires can't understand or learn without the help of the creator.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree that this is probably the case in general. However, what exactly are these &quot;telltales&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>You mostly nailed it, and no, there are no other telltales. In fact, it's easy to pass for a member of a clan by design.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Clan weakness is actually not that good of a tell because so many Flaws replicate them.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's worse than that, because most can actually be waived away, explained, performed by mundane means, or are simply hard to detect.<br /> <br /> <b>Brujah</b> still vary in frenzy frequency because of their Willpower; <b>Gangrel</b> will only develop bestial traits upon actually losing Humanity; <b>Malkavian</b> can have hard to notice Derangements and a too-weak-to-notice connection to the Network; <b>Toreador</b> only paralyze in front of art they really like, and only if they fail a Willpower roll; <b>Ventrue</b> must actually try to go beyond their preference to notice they're impacted, and a lot of vampires have preferences to begin with; the <b>Tremere</b> don't even have an actual clan weakness from a mystical POV.<br /> <br /> All that assuming the vampire just don't know, they're all pretty easy to pretend if it's an actual deception.<br /> <br /> The frequency of &quot;clan members&quot; that are actually <b>Caitiff</b> is completely up to the ST, and yes, it can be pretty high.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now you could go with the idea that besides showing the clan weakness herself, vampires also look to see whether her childer also show the clan weakness.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is indeed used as another &quot;test&quot;, but it isn't fail-safe either.<br /> <br /> On top of your considerations, and the fact that most vampires will go a large length of time without Embracing, <b>Flaws are also hereditary in the fiction</b>. Many Bloodlines may have started simply as a <b>Caitiff</b> with their own weaknesses, and even within the clans it isn't rare to have &quot;lineages&quot; traced through the Flaws in their Blood.<br /> <br /> Sure, not all Flaws are hereditary. But there's no formula to know which ones are and which aren't.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't have a problem with that. It's just that we never see any example of such things (probably due to a lack of imagination of the writers combined with them not wanting to confuse readers in a too convoluted backstory).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's exactly what happens.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only other telltale I can think of is their initial Disciplines.</div> </div> </div> </div>And it's exactly that.<br /> <br /> Disciplines are a strong indicator of one's clan, because the difference in XP does imply a big difference in ease to develop those powers, plus their &quot;intuitiveness&quot;: a vampire can almost always learn and develop their in-clans with effort alone, without teachers, not even their sire, the exception being Sorcery disciplines (within the fiction, Thaumaturgy isn't really &quot;in-clan&quot; for the <b>Tremere</b>, at least not in any verifiable way, they're just talented nerds).<br /> <br /> This isn't fail-proof, either, for a variety of reasons, but it does work more often than not.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, for people who don't know who their sire is, others can't use that. Many combinations of Disciplines might point to a certain clan origin which may or may not be true.<br /> <br /> So how do other Kindred react during this time period? Just treat everyone who does not have a known sire as Caitiff?\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the basic gist of it, give or take cultural bias, both local and clan.<br /> <br /> <b>Ventrue</b> usually rely on being able to recite your lineage back at least 3 or 4 Generations and don't bother with other questions. <b>Gangrel</b> and <b>Malkavian</b> tend to accept anyone who deals with their same shit. <b>Tremere</b> really care more about your loyalty to the Pyramid than anything, although talent with Thaumaturgy also weights a lot.<br /> <br /> Most clans will defer the identification of a member of another to purported clanmates of the potential <b>Caitiff</b>, so if the local <b>Toreador</b> took their word for it, or are simply absent, there <u>usually</u> won't be much more scrutiny.<br /> <br /> For whatever it's worth, <b>Gangrel</b> in some areas tell their clanmates about new childer, even if they don't tell the childe themselves. But that's about it, and it doesn't happen everywhere.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That would work if one knows one's sire, and the sire is looking after their childe.</div> </div> </div> </div>The participation of the sire is the real test in 90% of the cases, regardless if it works or not. It's possible even that some &quot;sires&quot; were actually adoptive, either due to personal interest or even to outright payment or whatever. While the official media of the game didn't really work much with <b>Caitiff</b>, the possibilities are many-fold, and a lot of vampires may have been erroneously classified one way or another.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552928#post1552928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are there any other possible telltales that I am missing? Or is it just Clan Weakness and initial Disciplines?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ease at developing a Discipline may be evaluated at a later time, too, but that's mostly that, yes. There are no other telltales.\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552933#post1552933\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I suspect that in the end whether one is treated as Caitiff or Clan boils down to how the Clan Elders treat you - the latter having long practice and long experience, their juniors will trust their judgement on the matter (or at least have serious reservations about contradicting the Old Guard where the latter can catch them out or snatch them up).<br /> <br /> As for the Elders themselves, I suspect that simple gut instinct (accurate or inaccurate) plays a role: if the Childe <i>feels</i> like one of the Clan then they will be treated as one of the Clan (Especially if they work to make themselves agreeable to their Clanmates and work to fit in).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, that's the most probable situation most of the time.<br /> <br /> Also, if a sire releases their childe as a formal member of the clan, others will probably be wary of calling the childe a <b>Caitiff</b>, depending on the sire's standing and power. Even a relatively innocuous sire would be able to pull a lot of weight around in this regard, simply because this is a particularly harsh accusation to levy around, regardless of evidence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-06T11:59:04-07:00","id":1552957,"title":"","content":"Thanks for correcting me about the Caitiff Discipline development mechanics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-12T05:54:41-07:00","id":1553088,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552902#post1552902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3- Only a few clans and bloodlines really have the means to readily identify someone isn't from their clan, much less a <b>Caitiff</b>, and the <b>Malkavian</b> actually aren't among them. From the Clans, old and new, only the <b>Nosferatu</b> can objectively tell someone isn't from their clan without magic, and even then only before 5th edition, now you can't even tell the difference between a Nos and someone really ugly;</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends a bit on the edition but Lasombra (no reflection) and some versions of Assamite (automatic diablerie aura) are also not that hard to check.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1552902#post1552902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4- The <b>Tremere</b> can divine someone's clan with magic, and <u>only them</u> can do so. Even other sorcerer vampires don't have access to this specific ritual.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not sure that's really the case. I don't think it the Rituals in the core book are also available to other types Blood Magic (at last with ST permission), and there are several types of non Tremere Blood Magic with access to the Path of Blood (I speaking of older editions no idea about V5). <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-12T10:46:06-07:00","id":1553099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553088#post1553088\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Depends a bit on the edition but Lasombra (no reflection) and some versions of Assamite (automatic diablerie aura) are also not that hard to check.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good points, although the Diablerie Aura is still replicable (with an actual Diablerie. You can Embrace a victim from time to time just to eat them if you have to pass as an Assamite). Getting rid of your own shadow would require specialized powers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553088#post1553088\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not sure that's really the case. I don't think it the Rituals in the core book are also available to other types Blood Magic (at last with ST permission), and there are several types of non Tremere Blood Magic with access to the Path of Blood (I speaking of older editions no idea about V5).<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><i>By the Lore</i> the Path of Blood and most rituals in the core are specific to <b>Tremere</b> magic, including this ritual. This did got a lot fuzzier in M20 with the new take on Blood Magic, and I like it, indeed, but technically a standard version of the setting will still have this limitation.<br /> <br /> It isn't a terrible difference, though, usually a lot have to transpire for someone to even care to make the effort of conducting this analysis with the blood of anyone else. Doing it to everyone Is a waste of resources, time, good blood that could be used for something better, and probably risk a lot of bad feelings, for absolutely no gain, since most people you'll test at random will simply be exactly the clan they're supposed to be.<br /> <br /> The most common way for a <b>Caitiff</b> to be examined is for them to actually pay for the service of learning once and for all if they belong to a clan or really are a <b>Caitiff</b>. And even then that would probably be expensive for the <b>Caitiff</b>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-13T11:45:16-07:00","id":1553144,"title":"","content":"In the Chronicles I played that had a Caitiff PC posing as a Clan member, the most dangerous thing for them was word of mouth across Domains. Basic etiquette for a new arrival was to announce themselves as &quot;X, Childe of Y, Clan Z of Domain A&quot; at the very least; most of them posed as Brujah, Gangrel, or Malk because they were less likely to be expected to rattle down several more generations. (Malks also had an uncanny nose for imposters, but most of the time just rolled with it to see what happened.)<br /> <br /> Anyways, the basic introduction was important because of a) status and b) paranoia for spies. If it's a known Sire, verification was as simple as writing a brief letter informing them that their Childe X had arrived in the Domain. If it's an unknown Sire, you could at least politely ask in their previous Domain. Their line is unknown, destroyed, or anything seems off (Auspex...)? Their status in the Domain is in doubt until someone verifies their story- that's usually the point where the Tremere came in for paranoid Seneschals or Princes. Impolite, maybe, but better than accepting a potential spy.<br /> <br /> Overall, most Caitiff that pulled through with posing as Clan members had to rely on a careful house of cards backstory that could collapse at any moment. It got easier once they established themselves in the new Domain, but the chance of a visitor pointing out &quot;that illegitimate Childe&quot; to the Harpy was still there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T10:33:54-07:00","id":1553174,"title":"","content":"I've always regarding being a Caitiff as being almost entirely political. It would be extremely rare for another vampire to truly know the Clan of a vampire when it isn't obvious like the Nosferatu. You know a vampire's Clan because you know who the vampire's sire is or you have some known member of the Clan who will vouch for the vampire's Clan membership. If the Malkavian Primogen says that you're a Malkavian, then for all practical purposes you are one - nobody is going to give you a psych evaluation to see if you're sane.<br /> <br /> So then why are there any Caitiff from a political standpoint? Can't they find someone to vouch for them or just fake it? Some probably can, especially older Caitiff who have had time to establish some contacts. But for young Caitiff, they likely don't have the connections to do so and they aren't going to find a Clan to vouch for them because of the Tradition of Progeny. If an established vampire claims a Caitiff as their childe, they become subject to execution for violating the Tradition of Progeny. Why take that risk for some Caitiff? Even Brujah Anarchs who might want to do this based on principle would be reluctant to take on that risk or responsibility. It surely happens sometimes and a certain percentage of vampires from various Clans likely are Caitiff but have been effectively adopted into a Clan.<br /> <br /> The best way to pull that off is probably to get the permission of a Clan Primogen who will vouch for the Caitiff as a Clan member who wasn't embraced in this city but comes from another location. Then the Caitiff presents themselves to the Prince a member of Clan whatever with the blessing of that Clan's primogen. If some vampire shows up and nobody knows who they are, no one vouches for them, then it doesn't matter if they only drink virgin blood or have a short tempter - they will likely be regarded as a Caitiff. That could be one of the rare advantages of being a Nosferatu. If you show up in a city and you're hideously ugly, everybody knows that you are a Nosferatu and members of Clan Nosferatu will immediately accept you as one of their own.<br /> <br /> None of this (aside from Nosferatu) has anything to do will &quot;biology&quot; or mechanics - it's all social.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-18T11:53:08-07:00","id":1553257,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553099#post1553099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good points, although the Diablerie Aura is still replicable (with an actual Diablerie. You can Embrace a victim from time to time just to eat them if you have to pass as an Assamite).</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure but Diablerie from time to time will likely have severe side effects.<br /> <br /> Blood Magic is anyway the only really fool proof way to identify a catiff, since most clan weaknesses are also available as flaw, and I think there was also in some book a flaw that lets a caitiff inherit the clan weakness of his sire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-18T15:56:31-07:00","id":1553260,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553257#post1553257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure but Diablerie from time to time will likely have severe side effects.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know. It's certainly a toll on Humanity, but not something you can't work with, you'd have a year before you have to do it again, IIRC.<br /> <br /> Other than that, the main consequence is exactly the black streaks you're after. There are vampires that live centuries with addiction to Diablerie, probably indulging more often than that.<br /> <br /> Aside being optional rules, the other effects of Diablerie are meant for the consumption of powerful, old vampires, especially ones with lower Generation. For someone doing it just for the kicks or something, it's probably kinda ok (as far as guaranteed sacrificing a Humanity dot goes).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-18T19:39:38-07:00","id":1553261,"title":"","content":"There seems to be a consensus that identification of a Caitiff (meaning a mechanical clanless with no clan weakness or clan disciplines, not a social outcast of a clan that shares the clan weakness and disciplines) is fairly hard outside of some obvious anomalies. If the only true way to identify a Caitiff is through a Tremere only ritual, that's not very good. The Tremere are a relatively small clan that has only been around for a thousand years for creatures that are probably at least eight thousand plus years.<br /> <br /> I still think some combination of observed &quot;clan weakness&quot; and the first Disciplines the Kindred demonstrates could be a sufficient tell tale sign. However, it doesn't seem most people think that's a strong sign.<br /> <br /> To me, that seems like a problem provided how the setting treats the Caitiff (as opposed to the mechanics). The setting seems to imply that Caitiff impersonating a clan is very rare. The setting seems to almost always have mechanical clanless Caitiff be recognized as Caitiff. Furthermore, the perceived &quot;menace&quot; of the Caitiff is something mentioned as far back as the Book of Nod, and destroying them is one of the primary (but not the only) function of the Scourge.<br /> <br /> I don't think this discrepancy is intentional. It's probably just the writers not thinking things through and simply defaulting that any mechanical Caitiff must be known to be Caitiff.<br /> <br /> If we are to prioritize the portrayal of the setting above the game mechanics, it seems to me there must be a stronger way to identify the clanless than what we're told. The alternative is to obey the mechanics and abandon the idea that most Caitiff are known as Caitiff, which means abandoning the concept almost entirely IMO.<br /> <br /> I wonder if a possible solution is to utilize some form of the Blood Ties/Sympathies or Predator's Taint rules in Vampire: the Requiem. Allow some vampires (perhaps based either on proximity of blood, old age, potent generation, or other marker ) to sense that the Kindred is, in fact, a true Caitiff. Or perhaps mark them as having an aura markedly different from &quot;true born&quot; Kindred so anyone with sufficient Auspex and training can know. It doesn't need to be a thing everyone knows how to do. If it is limited to real elders (in however capacity) word will quickly spread, at least in Camarilla society. Although I would want it to be fairly easy for Sabbat to identify as well given the internal dynamics of the Panders within that sect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-18T22:12:50-07:00","id":1553263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553261#post1553261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I still think some combination of observed &quot;clan weakness&quot; and the first Disciplines the Kindred demonstrates could be a sufficient tell tale sign. However, it doesn't seem most people think that's a strong sign.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's kind of a misinterpretation. At least some of the time, we were focusing on the idea of a <b>Caitiff</b> passing as a clan vampire in another Domain, or other fringe situations. Also, it's a matter of frequency, time and how deep the charade goes. We were also unsure about the goal, if it was to build upon misidentification being common or rare.<br /> <br /> Observed Clan Weaknesses and first Disciplines aren't <b>guaranteed</b> tell tales, because a lot of special situations can apply, but they're definitely <b>sufficient</b>, because most of the time you don't need exceptional accuracy. Specially given that most <b>Caitiff</b> have glaring gaps on their understanding of &quot;proper&quot; Kindred society.<br /> <br /> A regular kindred, even one without a Sire, will be observed by others in their city way before they know about the very concept of a clan or a <b>Caitiff</b>, so they can't really play others here. Those observers have the benefit of time, so Weaknesses and Disciplines can definitely be noticed and contextualized. Yes, there's a lot of ways how a Weakness or Discipline may give either a false positive or negative, <b>but most of the time it won't</b>.<br /> <br /> Flaws with similar effects to Clan Weaknesses <i>exist</i>, first Disciplines may be hard to identify, <b>Caitiff</b> developing a clan's spread happens, <b>but the frequency of all those things is on the ST</b>. The books don't really assume they happening with frequency. In fact, characters identified as having those flaws are exceedingly rare.<br /> <br /> You also define how much cities share news, depending on their distance and possible relationships between their vampires. The fact that a vampire arriving in a different city won't necessarily be investigated doesn't mean they won't <i>ever</i> be investigated. Each Prince is also different on their paranoia, so there definitely are some that won't rest until they know who exactly is a new arrival. And that given that a <b>Caitiff</b> manages to travel in the first place, as this means not only risking the usual dangers other vampires face, it also means the risk of being caught by other vampires away from protected areas, and simply not knowing for real what the dangers out there are (as they hardly have access to minimally reliable information).<br /> <br /> <b>Caitiff</b> also face the problem with how persecuted they already are. Being caught trying to impersonate a clan-vampire is a very efficient way to piss off everyone and give them an excuse to kill you in the process. So, on top of amassing all the conditions necessary to pull this off: fake a Weakness, have at least 3 dots in &quot;in-clans&quot; and preferably more, know the ins and outs of Kindred in general and the clan in particular, get to a far away place where no one will reveal you, possibly have to make an ally that vouches for you, on top of all that a <b>Caitiff</b> needs the courage to go ahead with this crazy plan.<br /> <br /> To make things even worse, any <b>Caitiff</b> caught doing this will put everyone on high alert. It will cause a ruckus in the Domain, and possibly in any Domain that gets wind of what transpired. This will, among other things, make things even harder for other <b>Caitiff</b> to try it.<br /> <br /> What comes to mind on the setting is that, while it is consistently stated that <b>Caitiff</b> always happened, old or low-gen <b>Caitiff</b> are really rare among canon characters. It's up to the ST if this means that old <b>Caitiff</b> eventually impersonate a clan, or if they eventually <b>become</b> clan-vampires somehow, or if they simply die too soon, or even if they just remain unremarkable, but are still there in the expected numbers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553261#post1553261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The setting seems to imply that Caitiff impersonating a clan is very rare.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sometimes it does, sometimes it doesn't, although it really leans more on the former especially as editions go.<br /> <br /> But how much ease or hardship do you need for this rarity? And how much you need it to be viable, even if rare?<br /> <br /> A more straightforward method for identifying <b>Caitiff</b> may make it actually unfeasible, even for a prepared person.<br /> <br /> Instead of frequency, you should think about how hard you want it to be for a character to actually pull it off, including the need for specialized supernatural powers, so you don't end up with a character facing more trouble than worth for the story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553261#post1553261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think this discrepancy is intentional. It's probably just the writers not thinking things through and simply defaulting that any mechanical Caitiff must be known to be Caitiff.</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably, but it makes sense.<br /> <br /> Most <b>Caitiff</b> will still develop wildly unexpected powers. Those without Sire will be promptly shunned by other vampires and denied education, while those with a known Sire are a minority, since most Sires would promptly kill such a Childe and try again. Without a well-established patron that can cover for them, a typical <b>Caitiff</b> would still have a very, very hard time passing for a clan vampire.<br /> <br /> And while it is possible, one thinking to be a clan-vampire while actually being a <b>Caitiff</b> would be exceedingly rare, maybe not something you see every century.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553261#post1553261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The alternative is to obey the mechanics and abandon the idea that most Caitiff are known as Caitiff, which means abandoning the concept almost entirely IMO.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily.<br /> <br /> Even if we assume that many, or even most <b>Caitiff</b> are passing as clan-vampires (knowingly or not), what makes for the concept to work is the fear other vampires have for something that defies their ideas of lineage.<br /> <br /> The point isn't how common is for a <b>Caitiff</b> to pass as a clan vampire, but how common <b>the vampire society</b> think it is.<br /> <br /> In fact, a chronicle where it is slowly revealed that the number of <b>Caitiff</b> around is far greater than previously supposed may have a lot of drama particular to it. That would be a big secret, with far-reaching consequences, and not necessarily one that anyone in particular is aware about in the first place (as those <b>Caitiff</b> do not necessarily communicate with each other).<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553261#post1553261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder if a possible solution is to utilize some form of the Blood Ties/Sympathies or Predator's Taint rules in Vampire: the Requiem.</div> </div> </div> </div>That would severely limit not just how frequent this happens, but what kinds of stories you can tell with this trope.<br /> <br /> Elders in particular have the problem that they're usually the ones most invested in the idea of killing <b>Caitiff</b> outright. Most Elders, if given such a power, would make for this trope almost impossible to apply within reach.<br /> <br /> Overall passing as a clan vampire, like any kind of infiltration or long-run deception plot, is a constant exercise. It has the aspect that any challenge is usually compounded by how frequently and thoroughly you have to fool people. Even when things are relatively easy, they don't stop to be a constant effort, and one that can be suddenly upset when you least expect it.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553261#post1553261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">it seems to me there must be a stronger way to identify the clanless than what we're told.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a final, but important note, I'd like to remind you that this isn't just about <b>Caitiff</b>. <u>Any method for recognizing <b>Caitiff</b> will also identify <b>any other impersonator</b></u>.<br /> <br /> While <b>Caitiff</b> passing as a clan vampire isn't something common in the official material, members of other clans doing it is. We have things like a <b>Lasombra</b> Prince passing as <b>Ventrue</b> for a long time. We have the <b>Baali</b> frequently infiltrating other clans. We have members of persecuted Bloodlines hiding their true lineage by passing as another clan, even cross-sect spies and defectors that do it to hide their original clans.<br /> <br /> Passing as a member of another clan isn't too hard, but making it so will have further impacts on how the setting works and what options particular characters have to navigate it.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-19T12:05:34-07:00","id":1553268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The point isn't how common is for a <b>Caitiff</b> to pass as a clan vampire, but how common <b>the vampire society</b> think it is.</div> </div> </div> </div>In terms of myself, I actually don't care about how many Caitiff are &quot;infiltrating&quot; a clan. I don't think that actually happens a lot. There's not many examples in the setting of this happening. I am actually concerned with how Caitiff are discovered initially - probably by their sires.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In fact, a chronicle where it is slowly revealed that the number of <b>Caitiff</b> around is far greater than previously supposed may have a lot of drama particular to it. That would be a big secret, with far-reaching consequences, and not necessarily one that anyone in particular is aware about in the first place (as those <b>Caitiff</b> do not necessarily communicate with each other).</div> </div> </div> </div>That is certainly a option. And if the ST wants this to be the case in their chronicle, so be it. Yet such a chronicle would be very different from the standard assumptions of the canon setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That would severely limit not just how frequent this happens, but what kinds of stories you can tell with this trope.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. However, in my experience at least, neither STs nor PCs are particularly concerned about telling stories of Caitiff not being identified as Caitiff. Everyone just accepts the standard canon setting that Caitiff exist and that most of them are identified as explicitly Caitiff. Your experience may be different.<br /> <br /> My playing group just accepts the standard setting assumptions that most Caitiff are identified. I'm just working out right now how that works in practice.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Elders in particular have the problem that they're usually the ones most invested in the idea of killing <b>Caitiff</b> outright. Most Elders, if given such a power, would make for this trope almost impossible to apply within reach.</div> </div> </div> </div>What trope is that? Do you mean the idea that Caitiff can infiltrate and disguise themselves as other clans? If so, yes, you are correct. However, I don't care about that.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Overall passing as a clan vampire, like any kind of infiltration or long-run deception plot, is a constant exercise. It has the aspect that any challenge is usually compounded by how frequently and thoroughly you have to fool people. Even when things are relatively easy, they don't stop to be a constant effort, and one that can be suddenly upset when you least expect it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, I actually don't care about Caitiff trying to pass as a clan vampire. So we're talking completely pass each other. I'm actually concerned with most Caitiff being recognized as Caitiff which is how the setting presents itself. Of course, if you (and other posters) aren't interested in that, that's fine too. You can run your games however you want based on your own preferences.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bAs a final, but important note, I'd like to remind you that this isn't just about <b>Caitiff</b>. <u>Any method for recognizing <b>Caitiff</b> will also identify <b>any other impersonator</b></u>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Only if you want such rules to apply to everyone, but that's not what I intended. When I mentioned applying some variant of Requiem's Blood Ties/Sympathies to identify Caitiff, I meant just that. Identify Caitiff. Not identify a KIndred's clan or bloodline in general. Kindred with true &quot;pedigrees&quot; - whether Brujah, Ventrue, Lasombra, Ahrimanes, or whatever - don't &quot;ping&quot; to that radar. It would just be something that being a mechanical clanless would somehow set off. Same thing if you go with an aura. There's something just unusual about them. Which I think fits the general elder paranoia that there is something <i>wrong</i> about the Caitiff. At least that is how I would run it in my chronicles.<br /> <br /> Of course, someone who wants to do something similar could use the same general idea to identify specifically one's clan, bloodline, or anything else. Whatever they think would improve their chronicle.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bWhile <b>Caitiff</b> passing as a clan vampire isn't something common in the official material, members of other clans doing it is. We have things like a <b>Lasombra</b> Prince passing as <b>Ventrue</b> for a long time. We have the <b>Baali</b> frequently infiltrating other clans. We have members of persecuted Bloodlines hiding their true lineage by passing as another clan, even cross-sect spies and defectors that do it to hide their original clans.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but as I explained above, this would still be possible in my conception of this house rule based on Requiem's ideas.<br /> <br /> However, to be honest, I wouldn't mind the idea that Kindred masquerading as another clan harder than the setting implies. IMO, it's kind of ridiculous how much infiltration can happen with fairly little effort in the setting. At least Marcus Vitel has the excuse he has a magical artifact that prevents easy identification. However, in terms of making clan identification fairly easy, I'm not sure if I would want that. However, to my point, that is not what I am suggesting here.<br /> <br /> Now in terms of Caitiff trying to pass themselves off as a clan, I don't necessarily want that to be impossible. Whatever might cause someone to identify a &quot;clanless&quot; doesn't need to be passive. It can be active. Furthermore, it doesn't need to be automatic detection. Some roll can still need to be required. Furthermore, you can even limit what Kindred can actually do that based on age, generation, proximity of blood, or other marker or combination of elements. Absent those conditions, a Kindred may not be able to do it at all.<br /> <br /> So it may still be possible for a Caitiff to not realize he is a Caitiff, or to slum it among some Brujah neonate anarchs and never be identified. But if a new Kindred shows up to Elysium or introduces himself to an elder Prince, then that might happen.<br /> <br /> If someone wants to implement that kind of house rule, the ST would need to determine how easy and frequently that could be done. Different STs would have different ideas on how much &quot;Caitiff detection&quot; they want in their game. Almost impossible without some kind of magical artifact or special Merit? Fairly common, but with noticeable exceptions? Hit and miss with some Caitiff not being identified for a long time, if ever? Or a sliding scale over time where Caitiff can frequently escape identification early on, but will eventually be found out? An ST can use various options. They just need to decide on what kind of &quot;triggers&quot; are used.<br /> <br /> I probably would use something more on the stricter side because so much of the setting is based on Caitiff being unfairly discriminated against, and that most if not all of them seem to be publicly identified as such.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553263#post1553263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bPassing as a member of another clan isn't too hard, but making it so will have further impacts on how the setting works and what options particular characters have to navigate it.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, if someone wants to make identifying someone's clan fairly easy, the ST needs to think through all the impact that decision will have on the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-19T15:09:36-07:00","id":1553269,"title":"","content":"Now I have a firmer grasp on what you want, so I'll keep it brief:<br /> <br /> I personally think the current mechanics are enough. There's room for a <b>Caitiff</b> to masquerade as clan, but overall the actual circumstances make it hard, and most of them are indeed publicly identified. The fact that impersonating is possible remains, but only goes so far.<br /> <br /> That said, a feeling of unease upon meeting them isn't a bad idea if you're thoroughly disinterested on working a impersonation plot. It may even reinforce the themes of dread and apocalyptic signs associated with them.<br /> <br /> What I would suggest them is to start focusing on the frequency of weird Disciplines. While from an OoC perspective the player buys Disciplines as they wish, IC <b>Caitiff</b> have little say on what they develop without a teacher, especially the first dots. Those Disciplines don't necessarily have to be possible for others to learn, so on making unique ones for NPCs you don't need to worry too much about balance, most of them would be somewhat weaker, but benefit from the surprise factor, while some may be strong. Outside that, Disciplines unheard of outside specific Bloodlines and Clans may also be common for <b>Caitiff</b> to spontaneously develop.<br /> <br /> Alone that would help already with both identifying them and making them scary without being necessarily stronger. Other than that I would lean on the idea of dread and unease. Instead of active, the perception of a <b>Caitiff</b> being around could be diffuse, but disturbing. It could come when Discipline powers are used, as minute changes in them that don't change mechanics or pinpoint the clanless, but that definitely worries the user about the (further) corruption of their Blood. I particularly picture <b>Auspex</b> sensing something in the area as an impossible sensation whose origin can't be determined.<br /> <br /> My suggestion on making it diffuse isn't to ease impersonation, though, but to play on the theme of unease. In practice, with <b>Caitiff</b> being generally removed from &quot;proper&quot; company, the mere presence of such tell tales would be enough to weed out them soon most of the time, as those symptoms would be considerably rare in a vampire's nightly activities or Elysium. It would also raise tensions when a <b>Caitiff</b> is brought to Elysium and make perfectly sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-19T17:19:47-07:00","id":1553275,"title":"","content":"The utter ignorance of the caitiff probably means that most give the game away before they even know that there is a game to give away. <br /> <br /> Those caitiff who are technically members of a clan, but don't display the clan bane probably get away with it for much longer, with the exception of Nos caitiff, who are going to be immediately obvious. <br /> <br /> But Ventrue, Malks, Torries, and Brujah might literally get away with it for centuries. A Caitiff who was embraced as a Ventrue would simply seem to be a Ventrue who was particularly good at hiding his preference, or who was lucky enough to have a broad preference, like &quot;American citizen&quot;. The Malks who advance in vampire society tend to be good at controlling their madness, so a Caitiff born to a Malk would simply seem to be really good at doing what the most successful Malks all do. <br /> <br /> A Caiftiff born to a Gangrel is going to have a hard time surviving without the camping-gear that is inherent in Gangrel disciplines. But since the Gangrel tend to be loners wandering rural interstates, those few Caitiff who do survive might not even be noticed as Caitiff. It's not like the Gangrel are going to study some neonate long enough to make sure he develops animal features when he frenzies. You were sired by Sylvia Slayer-of-Lupines? Good enough, welcome to the Gather.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T03:35:46-07:00","id":1553282,"title":"","content":"Speaking as an ST, here's my advice:<br /> <br /> You can impersonate a Clan as a Caitiff but I view this as a &quot;high risk, high reward&quot; scenario in terms of Caitiff unlife.<br /> <br /> 1. Most Caitiff don't get the chance because they don't know the system well enough to pull it off so they can't called a Caitiff before they identify themselves. After all, they usually can't identify their sires and if they can, their sire is a Caitiff or someone who denies them. When they can identify their sire, the mystical lack of a link is usually obvious (i.e. your sire is a Nosferatu and you don't look like a monster)<br /> <br /> 2. A Caitiff who is impersonating a member of a Clan has to probably make up a plausible lineage or have someone willing to lie on their behalf. &quot;Hi, I'm Dave Johnson. I'm a 13th level Toreador and my sire is Lily Rose. She's from the Anarch Free States. She's dead now and I want to join the Camarilla. No, you wouldn't know her.&quot;<br /> <br /> 3. It's easiest to impersonate Brujah, Toreador, Ventrue, Ravnos, Ministry, Banu Haqim, or Malkavians. All of them have ways of figuring it out, though. The Malkavians may actually just instantly know eventually. Gangrel might kill you outright if you don't get the animal parts needed but you will be permanently the enemy of a clan in a city if you are found to be an imposter and killing you is probably something most Princes would overlook (if they don't call a Blood Hunt on their clan's behalf)<br /> <br /> 4. God help you if the clan notices you're a little too sedate, you feed off of the wrong vessel, or you pass by an Andy Warhol original without the appropriate reaction. You also probably might have suspicions about your Disciplines. No one is going to accuse you of being a Caitiff on a whim unless they're an Elder but if it's an Elder, they can call in a Tremere to check you or some other test that will almost certainly fuck you up. <br /> <br /> 5. On the other hand, if you do know enough Kindred lore, you can probably pull it off. The higher generations are enough that a plausible story about outside of town will cover most bases. Even if it is conveniently unverifiable. &quot;The Prince had me Embraced to fight something called the Sabbat. I was let live because I survived or my sire died fighting in the war for New York.&quot; The thing is to learn that kind of lore, you probably have to have been a Kindred for a while and people will recognize you.<br /> <br /> 6. Being a Caitiff is social suicide in the Camarilla but not USUALLY suicide-suicide. While the usual scapegoats, the Anarchs treat them more or less fine and the Camarilla doesn't go out of their way to destroy them unless they need a scapegoat and they do that to Neonates, Thin Bloods, Anarchs, and each other anyway. Still, some will feel the desire to do it anyway because they can't deal with being an outcast.<br /> <br /> In my Chicago games, Gordon Keaton was actually &quot;adopted&quot; by the Tremere as a Lost member of their Clan by Nicolai's order and taught some basic thaumaturgy despite a Brujah sire and being a Caitiff--it was because Nicolai absolutely intended to use him as a disposable tool but he let him play Tremere for a time. After all, Gordon would do ANYTHING to avoid being exposed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T11:51:39-07:00","id":1553296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553282#post1553282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking as an ST, here's my advice</div> </div> </div> </div>I have a few questions for you since I can easily use Chicago canon characters as examples. I think it will illustrate some of the issues I see. I think everyone assumes if someone is listed in the sourcebook as Caitiff that it's known they are Caitiff unless it is otherwise said. The sourcebook seems to assume that as well instead of explaining things.<br /> <br /> 1) Why or when was Gordon Keaton identified as Caitiff? His sire is a known Brujah. He should have been originally considered a Brujah by the other Brujah. What made people consider him to be a Caitiff? Dooley's other childe Anita wasn't. Was it because he heavily developed an out-of-clan Discipline (Dominate) instead of the traditional Brujah Disciplines? That's the only way I can think of. It would be a lot better if the sourcebook was explicit about how Gordon was identified as a Caitiff.<br /> <br /> 2) Why is Neon considered to be a Caitiff? Damien knowns Balthazar is his sire. His one dot of Discipline is even one of the original Brujah Disciplines (Celerity). Even though we, the readers of the sourcebook know Neon is a Caitiff, in the Chicago setting itself, shouldn't he be considered to be Brujah until such time as events prove differently? Balthazar can deny being the sire all he wants, and among the Elysium crowd that can be sufficient to declare him a Caitiff, but the anarch Brujah should be treating him like he is a Brujah for the time being. Did you do this in your chronicle?<br /> <br /> 3) Why isn't Damien considered to be Caitiff? His sire (Critias) is unknown to everyone, including Damien. His Disciplines include Celerity and Potence, but also Dominate. Do we just assume he frenzies a lot and this combined with one of his clan Disciplines is sufficient to cause other Brujah to accept him as Brujah? Or is he considered a Caitiff in your chronicles (even though we know he is really Brujah)? (Interestingly in Neon's character description, Damien is referred to as a Caitiff.)<br /> <br /> 4) Is Maldavis considered to be a &quot;social Caitiff&quot; who is really Tremere, or is she considered an actual mechanical Caitiff? Dickie knows her sire was DuSable because he was specifically watching him as a known Tremere. She even has some strong Tremere clan Disciplines. So again, is this a situation where the Elysium crowd considers her a Caitiff because DuSable denies it so her sire is &quot;unknown&quot;, but the anarchs think she's an abandoned Tremere?<br /> <br /> 5) What is Gengis considered in your chronicle? Although he's shown in the Brujah section in CbN 1e, that is clearly a mistake. He and his sire Ethrica are clearly shown on the Caitiff lineage table and Gengis' character description even states he is Caitiff. While he does have the traditional Brujah Disciplines, his strongest Discipline is actually Auspex. In CBN 2e this is changed as all references to Gengis being a Caitiff is eliminated, his Brujah Discipline ranks are increased, and his lineage is changed to be on the Brujah table. However, I think the original intent was that Gengis was a Caitiff and his inclusion in the Brujah section was a formatting error.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553282#post1553282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After all, they usually can't identify their sires and if they can, their sire is a Caitiff or someone who denies them.</div> </div> </div> </div>While not knowing your sire is something some clans will use to determine if you are Caitiff (even if you aren't), this is certainly not true for others. The traditional Gangrel embrace usually has the sire abandon their childe. While they are supposed to keep them under observation that can easily go wrong. The Brujah are also very cavalier after the embrace with many childer being abandoned. Some of those end up classified as Caitiff, but others are not and accepted as Brujah. Why is that?<br /> <br /> Until the first time you actually see a Gangrel frenzy and develop an animalistic feature, there's no proof. And it could be a long time before that character frenzies, someone witnesses that frenzy, and what if the animal feature shows someplace not obvious?<br /> <br /> Some combination of clan weakness and Disciplines used are useful over the very long term, but it's not perfect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553282#post1553282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When they can identify their sire, the mystical lack of a link is usually obvious</div> </div> </div> </div>What exactly is this &quot;mystical link&quot;? Everyone knows the Nosferatu curse is an obvious sign, but that is not the case for other clans. Almost all clan weaknesses can manifest as a Flaw as well. Many clan weaknesses can't be easily determined except over a long period of time. Or simply needs to make a relevant Virtue check or spend Willpower in order to not show the weakness. We should see canon characters where this confusion comes into play, but I don't remember ever seeing that.<br /> <br /> If Lodin sires a Caitiff, how will he know it's a Caitiff? Some Ventrue have exceedingly generous feeding restrictions. If they happen to develop their first Discipline as one of the classic Ventrue Disciplines instead of one of the others, that should be all they need. Yet we don't seem to have any canon characters who are &quot;born&quot; Caitiff accepted as a clan member. The setting seems to just assume any Caitiff is discovered very quickly. Are there any actual canon characters who are Caitiff successfully impersonating a clan, or don't know they are really Caitiff?<br /> <br /> Prior to recent editions, Tremere didn't even have a clan weakness. It was all about being blood bonded to the Inner Circle. Does every unsanctioned Tremere embrace result in a Caitiff then? What would happen if DuSable got approval to embrace Maldavis and she was even sent to Vienna? Absent that Tremere ritual plenty of Tremere sired Caitiff would be considered Tremere.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553282#post1553282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">God help you if the clan notices you're a little too sedate, you feed off of the wrong vessel, or you pass by an Andy Warhol original without the appropriate reaction.</div> </div> </div> </div>None of those are actually good tests. A Brujah can have high Self-Control and high Willpower and frenzy less than a Ventrue or Toreador, even if he can frenzy more easily than he should based on the game mechanics. Venture feeding restrictions aren't immediately known and can be quite generous. Their feeding restriction might be so slight, like &quot;no animals&quot; or &quot;no cold blood&quot;, that it might not be discovered for a very long time. A Toreador might just think Warhol's &quot;art&quot; is actually crap and not deserving to be recognized. Some people think Jackson Pollock was brilliant and talented, others thing he's an overrated hack. Some found anonymous artwork is completely neglected until someone authenticates as a lost work of some famous artist, and only then does it get praised and go up in value. Van Gogh did not get any recognition until after his death. Art is very subjective.<br /> <br /> Could a Caitiff who has a mental illness be accepted as a Malkavian? Lots of Malkavians don't have dots in Malkavian Time to hook into the Madness Network/Cobweb.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T13:19:52-07:00","id":1553301,"title":"","content":"My take on the subject is it helps to remember that siring a vampire without the Prince's permission is something that is punishable by Final Death. Lacroix got treated as a joke for the game but he absolutely got away with it when he'd established his (temporary) praxis over LA.<br /> <br /> Thus if Theodore Dooley was found to have made Gordon Keaton, he would have been killed. Gordon knows that he's Dooley's childe and vice versa but the two pretend not to know one another since they both know that would be an excuse for Lodin to have them both executed. As is, Gordon is beneath Lodin's notice so he hasn't bothered. Later, Gordon is a dogspittle of the Tremere and unlikely to claim to be a Brujah,<br /> <br /> Nicolai might have been able to save Dusable but even he might have thrown his childe to the wolves when it was discovered she was a world-famous Anarch.<br /> <br /> Damien notably should be a Caitiff by the same rules because he was also abandoned by his sire. However, he managed to get acceptance as one of the Brujahh because of his immense strength and volatile temper.<br /> <br /> In my Chicago game, I actually did a short adventure about Gengis and his &quot;Clan Switch&quot; where he decides to become leader of the Anarchs of Chicago because he hates Anita Wainwright and Maldavis both.<br /> <br /> To that end, he hires the PCs to track down his sire and get her to confess to siring him. It was meant to highlight what a hypocrite he is with all of his talk of equality. Being a Caitiff was &quot;cool&quot; to Gengis until he needed to make connections with the more powerful members of the Brujah in Chicago.<br /> <br /> He was never &quot;mystically&quot; a Caitiff.<br /> <br /> My opinion is there's really two kinds of Caitiff with the fact that there's unidentified Kindred of the lineage and there's ones who mystically are just &quot;blank slate&quot; Kindred. Socially, the former can be fixed (by identifying your lineage with the help of the Tremere or a sire to claim you) and the latter can impersonate a Clan but the mystical part is just something that is.<br /> <br /> What causes one to be a &quot;mystical&quot; Caitiff? No one knows but I think the mystical marks of it are a lot more obvious over a long period of time with the razor sharp eyes of Kindred to begin with. One slip up is likely to get remembered far more than you'd ever think.<br /> <br /> For example: Yes, a very broad feeding restriction for Ventrue means that you're unlikely to be identified by that. However, what happens if they see you feed from ANIMALS? What happens if they note you've never displayed dominate, presence, or durability? What if you lack stereotypical traits that don't play into the stupid assumptions of the Kindred aristocracy?<br /> <br /> It's not evidence.<br /> <br /> But its SUSPICION and that could lead to looking into your background. Again, there's plenty of explanations...but Kindred have long memories and are the pettiest mofoers outside of high school you'll meet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T13:37:27-07:00","id":1553303,"title":"","content":"There's also the implications of the Clan Compulsions. My headcanon is that the Clan Compulsions subtly influence behavior even when they are not fully activated. That is, that a Gangrel has a subtle vibe of wanting to solve problems directly and animalistically even when it is not fully under the Clan Compulsion. <br /> <br /> That's just my headcanon, of course, but even going by what's been stated directly, the fact that the supposed &quot;Gangrel&quot; never displays that compulsion even after years of being in stressful situations in the coterie is going to be noticed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T17:38:26-07:00","id":1553309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553282#post1553282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When they can identify their sire, the mystical lack of a link is usually obvious (i.e. your sire is a Nosferatu and you don't look like a monster)</div> </div> </div> </div>In this particular instance, while the Nosferatu in the sire's warren would almost certainly be aware of one's Caitiff status, would everyone else? A newly embraced Nosferatu probably isn't hobnobbing with the upper crust among vampire society or hanging out at the Tremere chantry. The rank and file might just assume that they're not seeing the vampire's real face.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T18:12:52-07:00","id":1553311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553309#post1553309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In this particular instance, while the Nosferatu in the sire's warren would almost certainly be aware of one's Caitiff status, would everyone else? A newly embraced Nosferatu probably isn't hobnobbing with the upper crust among vampire society or hanging out at the Tremere chantry. The rank and file might just assume that they're not seeing the vampire's real face.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> And then my mind suddenly goes &quot;<i>If there's any kernel of truth to the legends about the Niktuku and their motives to hunt Nosferatu, how might the antediluvian react to the existence of such Caitiff, distant descendants of its blood that have somehow sloughed off their curse</i>?&quot; and possible plot seed is born.<br /> <br /> Doesn't even have to be about the Ante or the Niks per se, just some elder(s) or ancilla(e) developing weird ideas out of kindred conspiracy theories and crypto-history....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 08-20-2025, 09:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T20:51:27-07:00","id":1553315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553309#post1553309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The rank and file might just assume that they're not seeing the vampire's real face.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The only problem here (and I mean it non-ironically) is <b>Auspex</b>.<br /> <br /> It isn't a given, but by itself is common enough that someone would eventually be sure this can't be just <b>Obfuscate</b>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-21T21:47:28-07:00","id":1553363,"title":"","content":"That's why I brought up just what sort of vampires the character might be encountering in its nightly life. Yes, Auspex is a foil, but I'd say most characters who develop it enough to reliably penetrate Mask of a Thousand faces would be elders, or ancillae members of the clans who specialize in it. I don't see a Nosferatu Caitiff spending much time in Elysium or most other places where such characters congregate. Malkavians would be a wild card\u2014but then again, when aren't they?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-22T05:35:04-07:00","id":1553371,"title":"","content":"I think the thing about impersonating another vampire Clan is that it's not about the short term but the long term.<br /> <br /> Cities in VTM are less like cities and more like high school.<br /> <br /> You get to know the same people and a very small cadre of them for a very long time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-22T13:44:24-07:00","id":1553384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552894-how-does-one-recognize-a-caitiff?p=1553371#post1553371\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cities in VTM are less like cities and more like high school.<br /> <br /> You get to know the same people and a very small cadre of them for a very long time.</div> </div> </div> </div>With a caveat: you don't actually see all of them every night in the same places.<br /> <br /> Going several nights without meeting most of those people (or even none of them) isn't deemed uncommon.<br /> <br /> Of course, this is offset by the fact that you're locked with them for decades, centuries if you get lucky, so it's still a valid comparison.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-03T02:27:42-07:00","id":1553604,"title":"","content":"Also, it's not a trick that's unprecedented even for non-Caitiff.<br /> <br /> It didn't work out for Marcus Vitel after all.<br /> <br /> You'd think he'd be happy to be a Lasombra Antitribu prince but he had 2000 years of people fucking hating him and not nearly the web of supporters and pawns that most vampires his age did.","signatu